# Production Recommender System

End-to-end recommender-systems portfolio project built on MovieLens 1M. The project goes beyond a notebook benchmark: it implements leakage-safe offline evaluation, collaborative retrieval, cold-start routing, learning-to-rank experiments, diversity/novelty re-ranking with rollback gates, A/B experimentation, FastAPI serving, observability, artifact integrity, load testing, Docker and CI.

**Release:** `1.0.2`  
**Frozen serving model:** `recsys-m9`  
**Primary offline metrics:** `NDCG@10`, `Recall@10`, `Candidate Recall@100`  
**Evaluation protocol:** global chronological `80/10/10`, with test sealed until M10.

## Why this project is portfolio-grade

A production recommender is not just a matrix-factorization model. This repository demonstrates the full decision path:

- explicit implicit-feedback conversion and data quality checks;
- global temporal splitting to prevent future leakage across users;
- reproducible popularity/content baselines;
- PureSVD collaborative candidate generation;
- cold-user and cold-item routing;
- pairwise LTR as a real experiment, including the valid outcome that it is rejected;
- diversity/novelty re-ranking with relevance guardrails and automatic rollback;
- sticky user-level A/B assignment, SRM, confidence intervals, power/MDE, CUPED and guardrails;
- frozen serving artifacts with SHA-256 integrity verification;
- FastAPI inference, exposure/outcome event logging and experiment routing;
- Prometheus-compatible metrics, operational preflight, load testing, Docker and CI;
- a single final test evaluation only after the serving policy is frozen.

## System architecture

```text
MovieLens 1M
    │
    ▼
M0 ingestion + implicit feedback
    │
    ▼
M1 global chronological split ───────────────┐
    │                                        │ test sealed
    ▼                                        │ until M10
M2 baselines                                 │
    │                                        │
    ▼                                        │
M3 PureSVD candidate generation              │
    │ Top-100                                │
    ▼                                        │
M4 hybrid/cold-start routing                 │
    │                                        │
    ▼                                        │
M5 pairwise LTR ── selected blend = 0.0      │
    │                                        │
    ▼                                        │
M6 diversity/novelty challenger              │
    │ deployment gate failed → rollback      │
    ▼                                        │
Frozen production policy                     │
    │                                        │
    ├── M7 A/B experimentation harness       │
    ├── M8 FastAPI serving                   │
    └── M9 reliability / metrics / Docker    │
                                             │
                                             ▼
                                    M10 single final test
```

See [docs/ARCHITECTURE.md](docs/ARCHITECTURE.md) for the detailed online/offline design.

## Confirmed development results

On the validation development protocol, PureSVD became the retrieval champion over popularity:

| Metric | Popularity | M3 PureSVD | Lift |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| NDCG@10 | 0.1261 | 0.1342 | +6.36% |
| Recall@10 | 0.0477 | 0.0727 | +52.25% |
| Candidate Recall@100 | 0.2959 | 0.3245 | +9.67% |

The later stages intentionally preserve negative experimental outcomes:

- **M4:** metadata blend selected `0.0`; no forced warm-retrieval regression.
- **M5:** pairwise LTR selected `ltr_blend=0.0`; the ranker was not promoted.
- **M6:** diversity/novelty candidate improved diversity on tune but failed the later relevance deployment gate, so production rolled back to the exact anchor.
- **M7:** the A/B engine works end-to-end, but its generated uplift is explicitly `demo_only=true`; MovieLens has no randomized online impressions/clicks.

The sealed M10 test was opened once after the serving policy was frozen. Final held-out results:

| Metric | Popularity | Deployed policy | Lift |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| NDCG@10 | 0.1973 | 0.2117 | **+7.29%** |
| Recall@10 | 0.0535 | 0.0644 | **+20.21%** |
| Candidate Recall@100 | 0.2727 | 0.3116 | **+14.26%** |
| Catalog Coverage@10 | 0.0352 | 0.1455 | **+313.2%** |

The evaluation covers **992 warm-user/warm-item users** and uses the exact frozen serving artifact (SHA-256 `6847221923010464582cd83358963051d500f9f6ee36e647a20e13a8abd2a74f`) without refit or test-time tuning. Full results are in [docs/FINAL_RESULTS.md](docs/FINAL_RESULTS.md).

## Reliability results from the completed local run

The M9 in-process preflight passed artifact integrity, train-only provenance, warm/cold inference, zero errors and operational thresholds. A 500-request concurrent local HTTP benchmark on the development Mac achieved:

- **500/500 HTTP 200 responses**;
- **431.6 requests/second**;
- **p50: 36.4 ms**;
- **p95: 105.1 ms**;
- **p99: 144.3 ms**.

The Docker runtime hotfix in M9.1 was then validated with the same frozen artifact fingerprint and a live recommendation request inside the container.

## Quick start on macOS

```bash
python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
python -m pip install --upgrade pip
python -m pip install -e ".[dev]"
```

Download/prepare the public benchmark and reproduce the development pipeline:

```bash
python -m recsys_lab smoke
python -m recsys_lab m1
python -m recsys_lab m2
python -m recsys_lab m3
python -m recsys_lab m4
python -m recsys_lab m5
python -m recsys_lab m6
python -m recsys_lab m7
python -m recsys_lab m8
python -m recsys_lab m9
```

### Final milestone — unseal test once

Only after the serving policy is frozen:

```bash
python -m recsys_lab m10
```

The first successful M10 run evaluates the frozen production policy on test and caches the result in `artifacts/m10/m10_manifest.json`. Subsequent M10 runs reuse the cached final evaluation rather than rereading test.

## Run the API

```bash
python -m recsys_lab serve --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8000
```

Useful endpoints:

| Endpoint | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `GET /health` | liveness |
| `GET /ready` | strict readiness + artifact integrity |
| `GET /metrics` | Prometheus-compatible runtime metrics |
| `GET /v1/ops` | structured operational diagnostics |
| `GET /v1/model-info` | artifact/model/experiment contract |
| `POST /v1/recommend` | recommendations + exposure logging |
| `POST /v1/outcomes` | click/conversion outcome logging |
| `GET /docs` | Swagger/OpenAPI UI |

Example:

```bash
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1/recommend \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"user_id": 635, "k": 10}'
```

## Docker

The image uses the frozen M8 serving bundle and an explicit runtime config path:

```bash
docker build -t recsys-lab:1.0.2 .
docker run --rm \
  -p 8000:8000 \
  -v "$PWD/data/online:/app/data/online" \
  recsys-lab:1.0.2
```

Then:

```bash
curl http://127.0.0.1:8000/ready
```

## Quality gates

```bash
ruff check src tests scripts
pytest -q
python -m compileall -q src
python -m recsys_lab m9
```

The release archive contains no MovieLens data and no user-generated online logs. Data is downloaded locally from GroupLens; model/reports generated during execution remain local.

